{"animath":"0.1","canvas":{"height":540,"width":960},"kind":"concept","level":"secondary","scenes":[{"caption":"Plants make their own food","duration_ms":7000,"id":"s1","narration":"Green plants make glucose using light energy. They take in carbon dioxide and water, and release oxygen.","steps":[{"at_ms":0,"do":"show","shape":{"cx":140,"cy":120,"fill":"#ffe27a","id":"sun","kind":"circle","r":54,"stroke":"#d9a821","strokeWidth":3}},{"at_ms":600,"do":"show","shape":{"id":"sunL","kind":"text","size":26,"text":"Sun","x":108,"y":205}},{"at_ms":1400,"do":"show","shape":{"cx":640,"cy":300,"fill":"#bfe6b0","id":"leaf","kind":"circle","r":90,"stroke":"#2e7d32","strokeWidth":4}},{"at_ms":2200,"do":"show","shape":{"id":"leafL","kind":"text","size":30,"text":"leaf","x":610,"y":312}},{"at_ms":3200,"do":"show","shape":{"id":"beam","kind":"arrow","stroke":"#d9a821","width":4,"x1":220,"x2":520,"y1":170,"y2":262}},{"at_ms":4200,"do":"show","shape":{"id":"lightL","kind":"text","size":26,"text":"light energy","x":300,"y":185}}]},{"caption":"In: carbon dioxide and water","duration_ms":8000,"id":"s2","narration":"Carbon dioxide enters through tiny pores under the leaf. Water travels up from the roots.","steps":[{"at_ms":0,"do":"show","shape":{"cx":480,"cy":290,"fill":"#bfe6b0","id":"leaf","kind":"circle","r":90,"stroke":"#2e7d32","strokeWidth":4}},{"at_ms":1000,"do":"show","shape":{"id":"co2","kind":"arrow","stroke":"#5b7fa6","width":4,"x1":700,"x2":556,"y1":160,"y2":236}},{"at_ms":1800,"do":"show","shape":{"id":"co2t","kind":"text","size":26,"text":"carbon dioxide","x":640,"y":140}},{"at_ms":3000,"do":"show","shape":{"id":"h2o","kind":"arrow","stroke":"#3f88c5","width":4,"x1":700,"x2":556,"y1":440,"y2":352}},{"at_ms":3800,"do":"show","shape":{"id":"h2ot","kind":"text","size":26,"text":"water","x":690,"y":480}}]},{"caption":"Out: glucose and oxygen","duration_ms":8000,"id":"s3","narration":"With chlorophyll trapping the light, the plant builds glucose and releases oxygen.","steps":[{"at_ms":0,"do":"show","shape":{"cx":480,"cy":290,"fill":"#bfe6b0","id":"leaf","kind":"circle","r":90,"stroke":"#2e7d32","strokeWidth":4}},{"at_ms":1000,"do":"show","shape":{"id":"o2","kind":"arrow","stroke":"#7a9e7e","width":4,"x1":556,"x2":700,"y1":236,"y2":160}},{"at_ms":1800,"do":"show","shape":{"id":"o2t","kind":"text","size":26,"text":"oxygen","x":710,"y":150}},{"at_ms":3000,"do":"show","shape":{"id":"glu","kind":"arrow","stroke":"#8a6d3b","width":4,"x1":480,"x2":480,"y1":380,"y2":460}},{"at_ms":3800,"do":"show","shape":{"id":"glut","kind":"text","size":26,"text":"glucose","x":430,"y":505}},{"at_ms":5200,"do":"show","shape":{"id":"eqn","kind":"text","size":26,"text":"carbon dioxide + water → glucose + oxygen","x":120,"y":90}}]},{"caption":"Energy in, food out","duration_ms":6000,"id":"s4","narration":"Light energy powers the change. That is why plants are called producers — they make food for almost all life.","steps":[{"at_ms":0,"do":"show","shape":{"id":"r1","kind":"text","size":32,"text":"Light energy → chemical energy (glucose)","x":120,"y":220}},{"at_ms":1800,"do":"show","shape":{"id":"r2","kind":"text","size":28,"text":"Needs: light, chlorophyll, carbon dioxide, water","x":120,"y":300}},{"at_ms":3600,"do":"emphasize","dur_ms":1000,"target":"r1"}]}],"subject":"science","title":"Photosynthesis: how plants make food","topic":"bio-nutrition"}